<p>Based on the illustrated structure, which of the following statements correctly describes the geometry and bonding in the dichromate ion?</p>
      ]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>
The dichromate ion, \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\), is an oxoanion composed of two tetrahedral units sharing a single corner oxygen atom. The central chromium atom is in the \(+6\) oxidation state.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Analysis of Geometric and Structural Features</strong><br/>
- The bridging \(\text{Cr–O–Cr}\) bond angle is \(126^\circ\).<br/>
- The bridging \(\text{Cr–O}\) bond length is \(179\text{ pm}\), which is significantly longer than the terminal \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds.<br/>
- There are six equivalent terminal \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds (three on each chromium atom) having a bond length of \(163\text{ pm}\) due to delocalization of the negative charge and \(\pi\)-bonding involving overlap of oxygen \(p\)-orbitals with chromium \(d\)-orbitals.<br/>
- There are two bridging \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds sharing one common central oxygen atom.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Verification of Statements</strong><br/>
The structure contains two tetrahedra sharing one corner with a \(\text{Cr–O–Cr}\) bond angle of \(126^\circ\), consisting of six equivalent terminal \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds of length \(163\text{ pm}\) and two bridging \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds of length \(179\text{ pm}\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
The dichromate dianion consists of two tetrahedra sharing one oxygen corner with a \(\text{Cr–O–Cr}\) angle of \(126^\circ\), containing six equivalent terminal \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds (\(163\text{ pm}\)) and two bridging \(\text{Cr–O}\) bonds (\(179\text{ pm}\)).</p>
      ]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
