<p>Which phenomenon accounts for the near-complete overlap of the atomic radii curves of the \(4d\) and \(5d\) transition series from group 4 onwards (e.g., \(\text{Zr} = 160\text{ pm}\) and \(\text{Hf} = 159\text{ pm}\))?</p>
      ]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Trend Identification</strong><br/>
In the periodic table, atomic size typically increases down a group due to the addition of an extra principal electron shell. From the \(3d\) series to the \(4d\) series, an expected size increase is observed.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Analysis of the \(4d\) versus \(5d\) Curves</strong><br/>
However, the curve for the \(5d\) series virtually superimposes on the \(4d\) series curve from group 4 onwards (\(\text{Zr} \approx 160\text{ pm}\) and \(\text{Hf} \approx 159\text{ pm}\)). This anomaly is caused by the intervention of the fourteen \(4f\)-electrons that fill prior to the \(5d\) series (between Lanthanum, \(Z = 57\), and Hafnium, \(Z = 72\)).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>
Because \(4f\) orbitals have diffuse shapes, their mutual shielding effect against the increasing nuclear charge is very poor. As atomic number increases, the effective nuclear charge increases steadily, drawing the outer electrons inward. This regular contraction across the lanthanoid series—known as <em>lanthanoid contraction</em>—almost exactly compensates for the expected increase in size between the fourth and fifth periods.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
The virtually identical radii of the \(4d\) and \(5d\) series elements are caused by the lanthanoid contraction arising from the imperfect shielding of the intervening fourteen \(4f\) electrons.</p>
      ]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
