<p>In any series, the melting points of transition metals rise to a maximum around the \(d^5\) configuration and fall regularly thereafter, but marked anomalous dips occur in the curves. Which of the following statements correctly explains the anomalous dip observed at manganese (\(\text{Mn}\)) and technetium (\(\text{Tc}\))?</p>
      ]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Trend and General Principle</strong><br/>
The high melting points of transition metals are attributed to the involvement of a greater number of electrons from \((n-1)d\) in addition to \(ns\) electrons in interatomic metallic bonding. Interatomic bonding strength reaches a maximum near the middle of each series where the number of unpaired electrons is maximized.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Interpretation of Anomalous Dips</strong><br/>
In the plotted curves, while tungsten (\(\text{W}\)) displays the maximum melting point in the \(5d\) series, manganese (\(\text{Mn}\)) in the \(3d\) series and technetium (\(\text{Tc}\)) in the \(4d\) series exhibit conspicuous anomalous dips (minima).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Conceptual Cause</strong><br/>
Manganese has a stable half-filled \(3d^5\) subshell and \(4s^2\) configuration. These \(d\) electrons are held tightly in exchange-stabilized configurations, resulting in weaker interatomic metallic delocalization and unique crystal structures (non-standard complex lattice structure denoted by X in metallic lattice classifications). As a consequence, fewer valence electrons participate effectively in metallic bonding compared to chromium or vanadium, causing an abnormally low melting point.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
The anomalous dip at manganese is due to its stable half-filled \(3d^5\) configuration, which holds the \(d\)-electrons more tightly and weakens interatomic metallic bonding, coupled with a complex non-standard crystal structure.</p>
      ]]></text>
