<p>Based on the graph, which metal is the only element in the first transition series having a positive standard electrode potential (\(E^\circ = +0.34\text{ V}\)), and what chemical behavior results from this value?</p>
      ]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Graph Interpretation</strong><br/>
The graph plots \(E^\circ(M^{2+}/M)\) for the first transition series elements. All elements from \(\text{Ti}\) to \(\text{Zn}\) exhibit negative standard reduction potentials, except copper, which clearly lies above the \(0.0\text{ V}\) line at \(+0.34\text{ V}\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Underlying Thermochemical Factors</strong><br/>
The transformation of \(\text{Cu(s)}\) to \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\) requires high energy:
\[\Delta H = \Delta_{\text{a}}H^\circ(\text{Cu}) + \Delta_{\text{i}}H_1^\circ + \Delta_{\text{i}}H_2^\circ + \Delta_{\text{hyd}}H^\circ(\text{Cu}^{2+})\]
The sum of the high enthalpy of atomisation (\(339\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}\)) and high ionisation enthalpies (\(\Delta_{\text{i}}H_1^\circ + \Delta_{\text{i}}H_2^\circ = 745 + 1958 = 2703\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}\)) is not balanced by its enthalpy of hydration (\(-2121\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}\)). This positive enthalpy change renders \(E^\circ\) positive (\(+0.34\text{ V}\)).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Chemical Reactivity Implication</strong><br/>
Because copper has a positive standard reduction potential relative to the standard hydrogen electrode (\(E^\circ = 0.0\text{ V}\)), it cannot reduce hydrogen ions (\(\text{H}^+\)) from dilute non-oxidising acids (such as dilute \(\text{HCl}\) or dilute \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\)) to liberate \(\text{H}_2\) gas. It reacts only with oxidising acids like hot concentrated \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\) and \(\text{HNO}_3\), where the acid itself is reduced.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
Copper has a unique positive \(E^\circ\) value of \(+0.34\text{ V}\), which accounts for its inability to liberate hydrogen gas (\(\text{H}_2\)) from dilute mineral acids.</p>
      ]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
