    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Interpretation of Ionic Radii Variations</strong><br/>
The steady decrease in ionic radii of \(\text{M}^{3+}\) ions from \(\text{La}^{3+}\) to \(\text{Lu}^{3+}\) represents the regular lanthanoid contraction. In contrast, divalent ions (\(\text{M}^{2+}\)) have a lower net positive nuclear charge per electron and retain an extra electron, resulting in substantially larger radii that place them above the trivalent line. Quadrivalent ions (\(\text{M}^{4+}\)) possess higher effective nuclear pull and smaller radii, placing them below the trivalent line.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Electronic Configurations</strong><br/>
- Europium has atomic number \(Z = 63\). Ground-state neutral atom is \([\text{Xe}]\,4f^7\,6s^2\). Losing two \(6s\) electrons yields \(\text{Eu}^{2+}\) with the half-filled, highly stable configuration \(4f^7\).<br/>
- Terbium has atomic number \(Z = 65\). Ground-state neutral atom is \([\text{Xe}]\,4f^9\,6s^2\). Removal of four electrons (two from \(6s\) and two from \(4f\)) yields \(\text{Tb}^{4+}\) with the half-filled, exceptionally stable configuration \(4f^7\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Verification of Other Species</strong><br/>
- For \(\text{Yb}^{2+}\) (\(Z = 70\)): Neutral atom is \([\text{Xe}]\,4f^{14}\,6s^2\); loss of two electrons gives a completely filled \(4f^{14}\) subshell.<br/>
- For \(\text{Ce}^{4+}\) (\(Z = 58\)): Neutral atom is \([\text{Xe}]\,4f^1\,5d^1\,6s^2\); loss of four electrons gives the noble-gas core \(4f^0\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
Both \(\text{Eu}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Tb}^{4+}\) attain extra stability because each possesses a half-filled \(4f^7\) subshell.</p>
      ]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
