    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[
<p><strong>Step 1: Chemical Reactions of Lanthanoids</strong><br/>
Lanthanoid metals (\(\text{Ln}\)) act as strongly electropositive reducing agents and undergo characteristic reactions with non-metals under specified conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Identification of Individual Reaction Products</strong><br/>
- Reaction with Carbon (\(\text{C}\)) at \(2773\text{ K}\): Lanthanoid metals combine directly with carbon at high temperatures to form dicarbides having the stoichiometric composition \(\text{LnC}_2\). Hence, \([\text{P}] = \text{LnC}_2\).<br/>
- Heating with Nitrogen gas (\(\text{N}_2\)): Lanthanoids react with nitrogen upon heating to form mononitrides wherein the metal is in its characteristic \(+3\) oxidation state and nitrogen is \(-3\), yielding \(\text{LnN}\). Hence, \([\text{Q}] = \text{LnN}\).<br/>
- Heating with Sulphur (\(\text{S}\)): Lanthanoids react with sulphur to yield sesquisulphides of formula \(\text{Ln}_2\text{S}_3\) where \(\text{Ln}\) is \(+3\) and sulphur is \(-2\). Hence, \([\text{R}] = \text{Ln}_2\text{S}_3\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Verification of Other Branches</strong><br/>
- Burning in \(\text{O}_2\) gives the sesquioxide \(\text{Ln}_2\text{O}_3\).<br/>
- Reaction with acids liberates \(\text{H}_2\) gas.<br/>
- Reaction with halogens forms trihalides \(\text{LnX}_3\).<br/>
- Reaction with water yields \(\text{Ln(OH)}_3\) and liberates \(\text{H}_2\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Final Answer</strong><br/>
The products correspond to \([\text{P}] = \text{LnC}_2\), \([\text{Q}] = \text{LnN}\), and \([\text{R}] = \text{Ln}_2\text{S}_3\).</p>
      ]]></text>
